ANSIBLE INTERVIEW SCENARIO 1:
We have 2 servers at 2 different locations. Need to copy files from Server1 to Server2 using Ansible.

============================================================
BEST APPROACH
============================================================

Ansible normally works from a control node.

So the safest and most common pattern is:

Server1
   |
   | fetch
   v
Ansible Control Node
   |
   | copy
   v
Server2

That means:

1. Fetch the file from Server1 to the Ansible controller.
2. Copy the fetched file from the controller to Server2.


============================================================
INVENTORY
============================================================

inventory.ini

[server1]
server-a ansible_host=10.10.1.10

[server2]
server-b ansible_host=10.20.1.20


============================================================
PLAYBOOK
============================================================

copy-server1-to-server2.yml

---
- name: Fetch file from Server1
  hosts: server1
  become: yes

  tasks:
    - name: Fetch file from Server1 to Ansible controller
      ansible.builtin.fetch:
        src: /opt/app/config/app.conf
        dest: /tmp/ansible-transfer/
        flat: yes


- name: Copy file from controller to Server2
  hosts: server2
  become: yes

  tasks:
    - name: Copy file to Server2
      ansible.builtin.copy:
        src: /tmp/ansible-transfer/app.conf
        dest: /opt/app/config/app.conf
        owner: root
        group: root
        mode: '0644'


============================================================
FLOW
============================================================

Server1

/opt/app/config/app.conf
        |
        | fetch
        v
Ansible Controller

/tmp/ansible-transfer/app.conf
        |
        | copy
        v
Server2

/opt/app/config/app.conf


============================================================
RUN COMMAND
============================================================

ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini copy-server1-to-server2.yml


============================================================
IMPORTANT INTERVIEW POINT
============================================================

The Ansible copy module does NOT directly copy:

Server1 -> Server2

Normally:

copy:
  src:
  dest:

copies:

Ansible Controller -> Managed Node


Similarly:

fetch:

copies:

Managed Node -> Ansible Controller


Therefore the common approach is:

Server1
   |
 fetch
   |
Controller
   |
 copy
   |
Server2


============================================================
ALTERNATIVE: DIRECT SERVER-TO-SERVER TRANSFER
============================================================

If Server1 can SSH directly to Server2, you could also use rsync/scp from Server1.

For example:

---
- name: Transfer directly from Server1 to Server2
  hosts: server1

  tasks:
    - name: Rsync files to Server2
      ansible.builtin.command:
        cmd: >
          rsync -avz /opt/app/data/
          user@10.20.1.20:/opt/app/data/


But this requires:

- SSH connectivity from Server1 to Server2
- Credentials/SSH key
- Firewall rules
- rsync installed

Because of that, controller-based fetch + copy is often easier to manage.


============================================================
BETTER FOR LARGE FILES
============================================================

For very large files or directories, avoid unnecessarily moving everything through the controller if the network topology allows direct transfer.

You may use:

- rsync
- synchronize module
- shared object storage
- Azure Blob / S3
- NFS/shared storage

depending on the architecture.


============================================================
INTERVIEW ANSWER
============================================================

"If I need to copy a file from one managed server to another using Ansible, I normally use a two-step approach.

First I use the fetch module to copy the file from Server1 to the Ansible control node.

Then I use the copy module to transfer that file from the control node to Server2.

This is because Ansible's copy module normally transfers files from the controller to the managed node rather than directly between two managed nodes.

For large datasets, if direct connectivity exists, I may use rsync or the synchronize module to avoid routing large files through the controller."


