ANSIBLE INTERVIEW SCENARIO 2:

We have some packages to install on Server A and different packages to install on Server B. How do you manage this using a single playbook?


============================================================
BEST APPROACH
============================================================

There are multiple ways.

For a simple scenario, use:

- Inventory groups
- Separate plays in one playbook

For a more scalable implementation, use:

- group_vars
- package variables
- single generic package task


============================================================
OPTION 1: SIMPLE SINGLE PLAYBOOK
============================================================

inventory.ini

[server_a]
server-a ansible_host=10.10.1.10

[server_b]
server-b ansible_host=10.20.1.20


install-packages.yml

---
- name: Install packages on Server A
  hosts: server_a
  become: yes

  tasks:
    - name: Install Server A packages
      ansible.builtin.package:
        name:
          - nginx
          - git
          - curl
        state: present


- name: Install packages on Server B
  hosts: server_b
  become: yes

  tasks:
    - name: Install Server B packages
      ansible.builtin.package:
        name:
          - httpd
          - wget
          - unzip
        state: present


============================================================
FLOW
============================================================

Single Playbook
      |
      +----------------------+
      |                      |
      v                      v
Server A                  Server B
      |                      |
nginx                    httpd
git                      wget
curl                     unzip


============================================================
OPTION 2: BETTER ENTERPRISE APPROACH USING VARIABLES
============================================================

This is cleaner and more scalable.

Project structure:

ansible/
|
+-- inventory.ini
|
+-- group_vars/
|   |
|   +-- server_a.yml
|   +-- server_b.yml
|
+-- install-packages.yml


============================================================
INVENTORY
============================================================

inventory.ini

[server_a]
server-a ansible_host=10.10.1.10

[server_b]
server-b ansible_host=10.20.1.20


============================================================
GROUP VARIABLES FOR SERVER A
============================================================

group_vars/server_a.yml

packages_to_install:
  - nginx
  - git
  - curl


============================================================
GROUP VARIABLES FOR SERVER B
============================================================

group_vars/server_b.yml

packages_to_install:
  - httpd
  - wget
  - unzip


============================================================
SINGLE PLAYBOOK
============================================================

install-packages.yml

---
- name: Install required packages
  hosts:
    - server_a
    - server_b

  become: yes

  tasks:
    - name: Install environment-specific packages
      ansible.builtin.package:
        name: "{{ packages_to_install }}"
        state: present


============================================================
HOW IT WORKS
============================================================

Ansible checks which inventory group the server belongs to.

For Server A:

packages_to_install:

nginx
git
curl


For Server B:

packages_to_install:

httpd
wget
unzip


The task itself remains exactly the same:

package:
  name: "{{ packages_to_install }}"
  state: present


============================================================
WHY THIS APPROACH IS BETTER
============================================================

It separates:

CODE

from:

CONFIGURATION


Playbook contains:

HOW to install packages.


group_vars contains:

WHAT packages should be installed.


This makes the playbook reusable.


============================================================
EXAMPLE WITH MORE SERVERS
============================================================

Suppose later we have:

Web Servers
DB Servers
Monitoring Servers


Inventory:

[web]
web01
web02

[database]
db01
db02

[monitoring]
monitor01


group_vars/web.yml

packages_to_install:
  - nginx
  - curl


group_vars/database.yml

packages_to_install:
  - mysql-server
  - python3


group_vars/monitoring.yml

packages_to_install:
  - prometheus
  - node-exporter


One playbook:

---
- name: Install packages
  hosts: all
  become: yes

  tasks:
    - name: Install required packages
      ansible.builtin.package:
        name: "{{ packages_to_install }}"
        state: present


============================================================
WHAT IF SERVER A AND B USE DIFFERENT OPERATING SYSTEMS?
============================================================

The generic:

ansible.builtin.package

module is useful because it uses the system's package manager.

For example:

Ubuntu/Debian
    ->
apt

RHEL/CentOS
    ->
dnf/yum


Example:

---
- name: Install packages
  hosts: all
  become: yes

  tasks:
    - name: Install required packages
      ansible.builtin.package:
        name: "{{ packages_to_install }}"
        state: present


But package names may differ between operating systems.

For example:

Ubuntu:

apache2


RHEL:

httpd


In that case, OS-specific variables can be used.


============================================================
OPTION 3: USING CONDITIONALS
============================================================

You can also use:

when:


Example:

---
- name: Install different packages
  hosts: all
  become: yes

  tasks:

    - name: Install packages on Server A
      ansible.builtin.package:
        name:
          - nginx
          - git
        state: present
      when: inventory_hostname == "server-a"


    - name: Install packages on Server B
      ansible.builtin.package:
        name:
          - httpd
          - wget
        state: present
      when: inventory_hostname == "server-b"


This works, but for large environments I prefer inventory groups and variables instead of many hostname-based conditions.


============================================================
INTERVIEW QUESTION:
Which approach would you prefer?
============================================================

My preferred approach is:

Inventory Groups
        +
group_vars
        +
Reusable Playbook


Instead of:

Large number of hardcoded when conditions


Because it is:

- Cleaner
- Reusable
- Scalable
- Easier to maintain
- Easier for different teams to understand


============================================================
30-SECOND INTERVIEW ANSWER
============================================================

"If Server A and Server B require different packages, I can still handle them using a single playbook.

I define Server A and Server B in separate inventory groups and maintain their package lists in group_vars.

For example, server_a.yml may contain nginx, git and curl, while server_b.yml may contain httpd, wget and unzip.

Then the same generic task uses the Ansible package module with a variable like packages_to_install.

This keeps the installation logic reusable and keeps server-specific configuration outside the playbook.

For a very small setup, I could also use two plays in the same YAML file, but for enterprise environments I prefer inventory groups plus group_vars."


